# Public share link

Public access turns on a single read-only link that **anyone you give it to can
open — with no login and no invite**. Because there's no sign-in, treat the link
itself as the key: anyone who has it can see whatever you've chosen to share.

It is **off until you turn it on**, and you turn it on under
**Settings > Sharing & Privacy**:

<Screenshot
  id="sharing-public-link"
  callouts={[
    { anchor: "enable", label: "Turn public access on or off" },
    { anchor: "time-window", label: "How far back viewers can see" },
  ]}
/>

The address pictured belongs to a local test instance; yours will end in your own
domain, in the shape `https://<random>.share.<your domain>`.

## The link is shown once

When you first switch public access on, Nocturne creates the link and shows it to
you **that one time**. Copy it there and then, and keep it somewhere you can get
back to — a password manager is ideal. If you lose it, you can't ask Nocturne to
show it again; you **Regenerate** to get a new one, which also stops the old one
working.

The address is a long random code on its own web address, along the lines of
`k7m2p9x4q1.share.example.com`. That random part is the whole of the security:
there's no list of shares to browse and nothing to guess your way to.

## Choosing what's visible

You stay in control of two things.

**What's visible.** There are seven categories you can switch on or off
independently:

| Category | What it shares |
|----------|----------------|
| Blood Glucose | Your glucose readings and the graph of them |
| Treatments | Logged insulin doses, carbs and other entries |
| Device Status | The state of your pump, CGM and closed loop, including insulin on board |
| Heart Rate | Heart rate collected from a watch or band |
| Step Count | Step counts collected from a watch or phone |
| Food & Meals | Your saved food items and the meals you have logged |
| Reports | The report pages, such as the day-by-day and profile reports |

When public access is first switched on, only **Blood Glucose** is shared. Every
other category stays private until you deliberately switch it on, so a link you
turn on and forget about never quietly widens.

**How far back.** Choose _All history_ or limit viewers to the _Last 24 hours_,
so older data is never exposed.

<Callout type="tip" title="When to use it: a daycare">
  The daycare wants to keep your child's current glucose up on a tablet at the
  desk, and staff change shift through the day. Turn on public access, leave it on
  <em>Blood Glucose</em> only, and set the window to <em>Last 24 hours</em>.
  Everyone on shift sees the same screen with no logins to hand around, and they
  never see older history. If a device goes missing or the room changes,
  regenerate the link and the old one stops working.
</Callout>

## What the visitor sees

Someone opening the link gets a read-only dashboard: the data, and a **Sign in**
button where your own account menu would be. There is nothing on it they can edit
or log. It shows only the categories you switched on, within the time window you
chose:

<Screenshot id="share-anonymous-view" />

## Turning it off or replacing it

There's no expiry — the link works until you turn public access off. Two controls
end it:

- **Switch public access off.** The link stops working immediately.
- **Regenerate.** Nocturne issues a brand-new link and the old one stops working
  immediately. Use this when a link has spread further than you meant it to, but
  you still want the daycare (or whoever) to have one — you just have to hand out
  the new address.

<Callout type="warning" title="Anyone with the link can open it">
  There's no sign-in on a public link, and you can't control who it gets forwarded
  to once it's out. Share the minimum categories the situation actually needs,
  keep the 24-hour window on, and regenerate the link if you ever think it has
  spread too far.
</Callout>

If only one person needs a look, and only once, a
[temporary guest link](/docs/sharing/guest-links) is the safer choice — it expires
on its own. If they need ongoing access, give them their own
[member account](/docs/sharing/members).
